\title{Propositions and relations}
\import{prelude}

\p{There is an additional type-of-types \code{\kwi{Prop}} whose elements are simpler than those of \code{\kwi{Set}}; its elements are collections that themselves have \em{at most one} element. One can think of \code{\kwi{Prop}} as the type of [truth values](TODO). An element of \code{\kwi{Prop}} which is an inhabited collection corresponds to \code{true}, and an element which is an empty collection corresponds to \code{false}.}

\p{While on its own \code{\kwi{Prop}} is boring, the real power comes from combining it with function types. For instance, we can describe whether each child is nice or naughty. We have a single set (of all the children), and two families of propositions over that set: one for whether the child is nice, and one for whether the child is naughty. (In this schema, nothing stops a single child from being \em{both} nice and naughty.)}

\pre{%
\kw{theory} ChildBehaviour := \kw{sig}
  Child : \kwi{Set}
  is-naughty : Child -> \kwi{Prop}
  is-nice : Child -> \kwi{Prop}
\kw{end}
}

\dbexample{
  \p{\strong{Table \code{root.Child}}}

  \table{
    \row{\hcell{Row ID}}
  }{
    \row{\cell{\code{c1}}}
    \row{\cell{\code{c2}}}
    \row{\cell{\code{c3}}}
  }

  \p{\strong{Table \code{root.is-naughty}}}

  \table{
    \row{\hcell{\code{a : root.Child}}}
  }{
    \row{\cell{\code{c1}}}
    \row{\cell{\code{c3}}}
  }

  \p{\strong{Table \code{root.is-nice}}}

  \table{
    \row{\hcell{\code{a : root.Child}}}
  }{
    \row{\cell{\code{c2}}}
    \row{\cell{\code{c3}}}
  }

  \p{\strong{Constraints:}}
  \ul{
    \li{Each value in column \code{a} of either relation table is a row ID in \code{root.Child}.}
    \li{Each child appears at most once in column \code{a} of each relation table.}
  }
}

\p{Each of \code{is-naughty} and \code{is-nice} are lowered to their own database table, similarly to fields that end in \code{\kwi{Set}}. The only difference is that a primary key constraint is added, enforcing that each child has \em{at most one} row claiming that he/she \code{is-nice}, and \em{at most one} row claiming that he/she \code{is-naughty}.}

\p{Just as we saw in [[001D]], we can specify theories that demand that the sets/propositions we've declared actually have some inhabitants:}

\pre{
\kw{theory} ChildBehaviour := \kw{sig}
  Child : \kwi{Set}
  is-naughty : Child -> \kwi{Prop}
  is-nice : Child -> \kwi{Prop}

  veruca : Child
  spoilt-rotten : is-naughty veruca

  charlie : Child
  returned-gobstopper : is-nice charlie
\kw{end}
}

\dbexample{
  \p{Keep the three tables from the preceding instance and add:}

  \p{\strong{Table \code{root.veruca}}}

  \table{
    \row{\hcell{\code{a : root.Child}}}
  }{
    \row{\cell{\code{c1}}}
  }

  \p{\strong{Table \code{root.charlie}}}

  \table{
    \row{\hcell{\code{a : root.Child}}}
  }{
    \row{\cell{\code{c2}}}
  }

  \p{\strong{Additional constraints:}}
  \ul{
    \li{The \code{root.veruca} and \code{root.charlie} tables each have exactly one row.}
    \li{Each value in column \code{a} of these tables is a row ID in \code{root.Child}.}
    \li{The value in \code{root.veruca.a} appears in column \code{a} of \code{root.is-naughty}.}
    \li{The value in \code{root.charlie.a} appears in column \code{a} of \code{root.is-nice}.}
  }
}

\p{Here we see an important optimisation applied by the Coln compiler. When an \em{element} of \code{\kwi{Prop}} is used as a field, or more generally as the input or output type of a function, Coln converts the field into a constraint rather than an additional table to be stored. So \code{spoilt-rotten} becomes a constraint that forces \code{is-naughty} to contain \code{veruca} in the relation, but does not have to itself be stored as a table.}

\p{Note that this doesn't apply to \code{is-naughty} and \code{is-nice} themselves; they are functions into \code{\kwi{Prop}} itself, and not functions into propositions.}

\p{We call a function whose codomain is \code{\kwi{Prop}} a \defcase{relation}. Here, \code{is-naughty} and \code{is-nice} are \em{unary} relations, because each takes in one argument. Here is another example, this time of a \em{binary} relation.}

\pre{%
\kw{theory} Family := \kw{sig}
  Person : \kwi{Set}
  is-descendant-of : Person -> Person -> \kwi{Prop}
\kw{end}
}

\dbexample{
  \p{\strong{Table \code{root.Person}}}

  \table{
    \row{\hcell{Row ID}}
  }{
    \row{\cell{\code{p1}}}
    \row{\cell{\code{p2}}}
    \row{\cell{\code{p3}}}
  }

  \p{\strong{Table \code{root.is-descendant-of}}}

  \table{
    \row{\hcell{\code{a : root.Person}} \hcell{\code{b : root.Person}}}
  }{
    \row{\cell{\code{p1}} \cell{\code{p2}}}
    \row{\cell{\code{p1}} \cell{\code{p3}}}
    \row{\cell{\code{p2}} \cell{\code{p3}}}
  }

  \p{\strong{Constraints:}}
  \ul{
    \li{Each value in columns \code{a} and \code{b} is a row ID in \code{root.Person}.}
    \li{Each pair of people appears at most once in \code{root.is-descendant-of}.}
  }
}

\p{An element of a proposition can be thought of as a witness that the
proposition is true. We can use this to put \em{laws} in our database.
For instance, we can require that the descendant relation is transitive in any valid instance of the schema:}

\pre{%
\kw{theory} TransitiveFamily := \kw{sig}
  Person : \kwi{Set}
  is-descendant-of : Person -> Person -> \kwi{Prop}

  is-descendant-of/is-transitive : (a b c : Person) -> is-descendant-of a b -> is-descendant-of b c -> is-descendant-of a c
\kw{end}
}

\dbexample{
  \p{The tables and constraints of the previous instance, plus:}

  \p{\strong{Constraints:} Whenever the relation contains \code{(x, y)} and \code{(y, z)}, it must also contain \code{(x, z)}.}
}

\p{If we read the third field in words, this says that for any three people \code{a}, \code{b}, \code{c}, if the propositions "\code{a} is a descendant of \code{b}" and "\code{b} is a descendant of \code{c}" hold, then "\code{a} is a descendant of \code{c}" holds. Because the output of this field is in \code{\kwi{Prop}}, the field is transformed into a constraint and not materialised as a table.}

\p{Coln does not automatically derive all facts that follow from a collection of laws; this law becomes a constraint on the user-supplied instances that the Coln database will accept. (But see \mvrnote{TODO} later for how we \em{can} derive facts like this automatically!)}
